ASN.1X TER (Text Encoding Rules)

The file is a ASCII (or Extended ASCII) text file.


=== Schema-independent format ===

The schema-independent format is the subset of the other formats, so a
valid schema-independent TER file is also valid for schema-dependent TER.
The same tokens are defined in schema-independent format even though some
of them are only used in the other formats, though.

Comments start with % (outside of a string) and end at the end of line.

Delimiters:
  {   Begin schema-specific block
  }   End schema-specific block
  [   Begin sequence
  ]   End sequence
  <[  Begin set
  ]>  End set
  <<  Begin key/value list
  >>  End key/value list
  <{  Reserved
  }>  Reserved

Schema-specific blocks can only be used in the schema-specific format, as
well as some items that are not specific to the schema which are mentioned
below as using the { } block.

Integers can be one of the following forms:

* Plain decimal integer, with an optional + or - sign at first.

* Integer with a base 2 to 36 (in decimal) and then # and then the number
represented using that base. Letters can be uppercase or lowercase, and
you can have optional + or - after # to indicate the sign.

A object identifier is represented by the numbers in decimal form with dots
in between. A relative object identifier has the same format but ... is at
the beginning.

Real numbers can be one of the following forms:

* Decimal, with or without a 10# prefix, and with or without a fractional
part (with a dot before it) and an exponent part (with e or E before it).
At least two of these three parts must be present (in addition to the
integer part), in order to be distinguished from object identifiers. It
may also have a - or + sign (after the 10# prefix if it is present), in
which case you may have . without the exponent and base.

* Binary, using the prefix 2# or 4# or 8# or 16# for the base of the
significand, and the exponent part has p or P before it and is always
written in decimal form and is the number of bits (regardless of the
base of the representation). You must have at least one of the fractional
part of the significand and the exponent part (you can have both), in
order to be distinguished from integers. It may also have a - or + sign
(after the prefix), in which case . without the exponent is possible.

Both integers and real numbers can include optional underscores (but not
as the first character) which have no meaning.

Special values:
  #Z     Null (same as "5U<>")
  #F     False (same as "1U<00>")
  #T     True (same as "1U<FF>")
  #INF   Positive infinity
  #+INF  Positive infinity
  #-INF  Negative infinity
  #NAN   Not a number

An implicit type is specified by a token consisting of the type number in
decimal form, followed by one of U (universal), A (application), C (context
specific), or P (private), and has an optional colon afterward. This is a
single token, but the value that is represented by the next token or block,
will use the specified implicit type instead of its actual type.

An octet string token has < > around it, and contains the hexadecimal data
of the octet string. Spaces, tabs, and line breaks are allowed inside, but
not between the two hex digits that represent one byte.

An octet string can also use <= => around it if it is base 64. Trailing
equal signs are optional.

Text strings use ( ) around it. If the type is not specified, then IA5 is
assumed by default.

The possible escape sequences in text strings are:

* \\ \( \) represents the second character literally. Unescaped ( ) are
allowed if they are balanced.

* \a \b \t \n \v \f \r represents ASCII control characters, which are the
same as the C programming language.

* \e represents a ASCII escape character, as in GNU C.

* \x and exactly two hex digits represents one byte. This is not allowed
at all in UTF-16 strings, universal strings, and BCD strings. In TRON
strings it is only allowed for representing control characters (a space
is considered to be a control character in TRON strings).

* \ and exactly three octal digits represents one byte. It has the same
restrictions as the \x escape.

* \T and any number of hex digits followed by a semicolon represents a
single TRON character; it is only allowed in a TRON string. If the plane
number is not specified, then the current plane number is used.

* \u and exactly four hex digits represents a single Unicode character,
and is allowed only in Unicode strings.

* \U and any numbef of hex digits followed by a semicolon is like \u (and
has the same restrictions) but can represent non-BMP characters too. In a
UTF-16 string it must be in the Unicode range, but in a UTF-8 string or a
UTF-32 string, it can be up to 32-bits.

* \c and \g and \G are reserved for future definition to use with ISO 2022.

* \Z reverts the C0 and G0 sets to the original selection at the next
appropriate time.

* \ followed by any number of spaces, tabs, and/or line breaks, and then
one semicolon, represents nothing; it can be used to divide text by lines
in the text representation.

The following prefixes can be used to specify string types:
  OCTET:       Octet string
  O:           Octet srting
  IA5:         IA5 string
  ASCII:       IA5 (ASCII) string
  A:           IA5 (ASCII) string
  PRINTABLE:   Printable string
  P:           Printable string
  VISIBLE:     Visible string
  V:           Visible string
  GRAPHIC:     Graphic string
  GR:          Graphic string
  GENERAL:     General string
  GEN:         General string
  UTF8:        UTF-8 string
  BMP:         BMP string
  UTF16:       UTF-16 string
  UNIVERSAL:   Universal string
  TELETEX:     Teletex string
  VIDEOTEX:    Videotex string
  NUMERIC:     Numeric string
  BCD:         BCD string
  PC:          PC string
  TRON:        TRON string
  DESCRIPTOR:  Object descriptor
  DESC:        Object descriptor

Any of these prefixes can also be used with a octet string, in which case
it is like a implicit type.

Date/time tokens are one of the following (including the colon) followed
by the date/time representation in the same token.
  TIME:       UTCTime or GeneralizedTime
  UTCTIME:    UTCTime
  UT:         UTCTime
  GENTIME:    GeneralizedTime
  GT:         GeneralizedTime
  TS:         UTC timestamp

The date/time representation is same like that of the UTCTime and
GeneralizedTime types, but you can optionally put : and - and T in
the appropriate places (which will be removed). The separation between
the seconds and fractional seconds must use a dot and cannot use a comma.

There are also prefixes for the C floating point types (which are encoded
as the ASN.1 REAL type in binary format, even though the numbers will be
specified in decimal format in the TER). The SINGLE: and DOUBLE: prefixes
are used for this purpose.

A name token (which is only used for the schema-specific variant and for
macros) consists of uppercase and lowercase letters, digits, hyphen,
underscore, and must start with a letter or underscore. A name token can
also optionally start with a dot (in which case the next character must
be a letter or underscore), which is meaningful in some contexts.

The token BITS: followed by a { } block containing integers represents a
bit string with only the specified bits set (0 means the first bit). It
cannot contain negative integers or any values other than integers.

Another way to make a bit string is with <+ +> around it, and then after
the first plus sign must be the base number with # afterward; the base
number can be 2, 4, 8, or 16. It is then followed by the data, which is
like a < > octet string but represents bits instead, so spaces are allowed
to intervene anywhere, and the base is not necessarily hexadecimal. If the
base is omitted then base 2 is used.

The token ~ means that the following value is wrapped in a octet string.

A field number token (used only in schema definitions) is $ and then a
decimal number 0 to 65535.


=== Definitions ===

A name that has not been defined yet can be defined by writing = after the
name, and then the definition. The possible kind of definitions are:

* Object: The definition must be a OID. Then, this name can be used in most
places where a OID is expected, including in another definition. It can also
be followed by . and further numbers, to make a longer OID.

* Schema: See the below section about schema definitions.


=== Schema definitions ===

The schema definition is defined in two parts, being the input part and the
output part, in that order.

The input part is one of the following three types of blocks:

* { } means that the value is any one of the fields.

* [ ] means that the values are the fields in order (although some of the
fields might be optional).

* << >> means a key/value list; there must be exactly two fields (the key
field and then the value field), and they cannot be named fields.

Any of these blocks consists of the same things inside of them, which are
the list of fields in the order that they are expected. The definition of
each field consists of the following parts in this order:

* An optional asterisk, which is only allowed up to once and only in a [ ]
schema. This means that all following fields can occur zero or more times.

* The string type, which is optional and is one of the string type prefixes
mentioned in the above section. This causes any text string used as the
value to use the specified type by default if not specified with the value,
instead of using IA5: by default. This may also be OID: in which case the
value must be specified as an object identifier.

* The field designation, which can either be a name (which must be a name
that is not used for any other kind of definition, although it may match
the name of a field in another schema definition), or a field number.

* An optional question mark, indicating that this field is optional. A
field in a key/value list cannot be optional. A field with a default value
or required value is always optional (the question mark is redundant).

* The optional constraints, which is a { } block; see below for the
definition of constraints.

* The optional value. This is a ASN.1 value as in the schema-independent
format, with a = or ^ prefix. The = prefix means that if this field is
present then it always has this value, and it is not specified in the
data that uses this schema. The ^ prefix means that it is a default value
which is used if this field is not present in the data. Neither is allowed
in a << >> schema, and only = is allowed in a { } schema, but both are
allowed in a [ ] schema.

Constraints are any combination of the following:

* Any name, which does not need to be defined ahead of time (but it must
be defined before any data that uses this schema is defined). If the
definition of that name is a OID, then this field will be encoded as
relative to the specified OID if possible (but it will be encoded as
absolute if it cannot be encoded as relative). If the definition of that
name is a schema, then the value always uses that schema and the name of
that schema does not need to be specified with the value when making a
data item using this schema.

* OF: followed by a string, which restricts the set of characters that
are allowed in this field. This is only valid for the ASCII-based string
types, as well as PC String, BCD String, and Octet String; and it requires
that the string type is specified for this field. It also requires that
the type of the field is the specified string type. There is a special
handling of this string; the character = means a range between the
preceding and following character, but \= can mean a literal equal sign.
When restricting the set of characters of a BCD string, a exclamation
mark means that a optional trailing space is allowed.

* TYPE: followed by a implicit type token or a { } block containing
implicit type tokens. Requires the value of this field to be of one
of the specified types.

* VMIN: or VMAX: followed by a value means the minimum or maximum value.
The possible types are integer, enumerated, and real.

* SMIN: or SMAX: followed by a nonnegative integer means the minimum or
maximum size of the data in bytes.

Constraints are ignored if the default value of a field is used.

If the schema type is << >> then the output part is omitted, otherwise
the output part is a single output item, where a output item is one of:

* A field designation (name or number). It is OK to have a field multiple
times. If the field is not present in the data and no default value is
defined, then it will be omitted from the output (in some cases it will
result in a null value instead, if it cannot be omitted).

* An asterisk. For a [ ] schema, this means all fields that follow the
asterisk in the input part will be included, as many times as they are.
For a { } schema, it means whichever field is selected.

* A [ ] or <[ ]> block containing zero or more output items; this will
make a sequence or set containing the data.

* An implicit type token, followed by another output item, in which
case it will be encoded as that implicit type.

* The sign ~ and a output item, which wraps the value.

Any output item inside of a [ ] or <[ ]> block may optionally have ^
afterward followed by a value, in which case that field is omitted from
the output if its value is equal to the specified value. This is not
allowed if the item is itself a [ ] or <[ ]> block, though.

When mentioning the schema within the data, it works by writing the name
of the schema followed by:

* If the schema type is { } then any of its field names (followed by the
value if it is not implied), with optionally { } around it; there must be
exactly one of them. If it is a numbered field, then the field number is
omitted and only the value is necessary.

* If the schema type is [ ] then you need { } around the list of the
fields, each of which is specified like for a { } schema type (but
without the { } around each one individually).

* If the schema type is << >> then you need either { } or << >> around
the list of key/value pairs, like an ordinary << >> data item, but the
fields can be handled like the { } and [ ] schema type.

